The idea of the new simulation is as follows:

1. Place the first finger on the target object;
2. Place the second finger anywhere on the screen (not necessarily on the black bar);
3. Now you can remove the first finger from the screen.

This implementation looks more familiar and doesn't require to use black bars for the correct operation. ~It has one flaw though - it doesn't currently support the simulation of moving the mouse with the right button pressed, but, AFAIK, it's not used in the game anyway~ no longer true.

Also this implementation fixes the flaw in the logic of the current implementation I encountered. This logic:

https://github.com/ihhub/fheroes2/blob/1aa3c433b78eedf7c54d89aabb32c14e9019911b/src/engine/localevent.cpp#L1368-L1387

may not work in case of three (or more) fingers and `_numTouches` may become negative. Consider the following situation:

1. I touch the screen by the first finger. The initial check passes because `_numTouches` is `0`, now `_numTouches` is set to `1` and `_firstFingerId` is set to the first finger's id;
2. I touch the screen by the second finger. The initial check passes because `_numTouches` is `1`, now `_numTouches` is set to `2` and `_secondFingerId` is set to the second finger's id;
3. I touch the screen by the third finger. The initial check fails because `_numTouches` is `2` and third finger's id is neither `_firstFingerId` nor `_secondFingerId` and `return` is called. So far so good.
4. I remove the first finger from the screen. The initial check passes because  `_numTouches` is `2`, but first finger's id equals the `_firstFingerId`, now `_numTouches` is set to `1`;
5. I remove the second finger from the screen. The initial check passes because  `_numTouches` is `1`, now `_numTouches` is set to `0`;
6. Now things getting interesting. I remove the third finger from the screen. The initial check passes because  `_numTouches` is `0`, now `_numTouches` is set to `-1` and we are in a complete mess.

